WEBVTT

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:07.540
So how much is your grid like Claude or ChatGPT? Here's which parts are the same, and which aren't.

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:22.560
The shared part is the loop. Text goes in. The model assigns every next token a chance. One is drawn, written down, and round it goes again. Your grid does that. So do Claude and ChatGPT.

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:30.960
We can see a limit of counting in the size of the table. Your magpie grid has five hundred and seventy-six boxes.

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:40.160
Frankenstein has seven thousand different words. That's forty-nine million boxes. And look how few have anything in them.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:52.100
Imagine a vocabulary of a hundred thousand tokens: words and bits of words. A grid for every pair would need ten billion boxes, before we even add more context.

00:00:53.610 --> 00:01:04.300
Your grid only looks at the last word. A large language model uses the text available in its context window. That can include earlier messages, but there's a limit to how much fits.

00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:26.540
Here's Frankenstein, looking back one word. Then two. Then five. In this last sample, every word comes straight from the book. More context can also mean more copying.

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:44.800
A large language model learns by nudging its weights to predict better, and those weights can share what they learn. Look at the magpie: the rows for "dog" and "postie" are exactly the same. Our grid stores them separately. A model that spots this can guess about the postie from what it saw the dog do.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:02:00.640
It uses tokens, which can be whole words or pieces. Here, "magpie" splits into "mag" and "pie". "Unexpectedly" stays whole. The splits depend on the tokeniser.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:14.100
After learning from reading, a model can get further training. People write example answers, and provide feedback by rating candidate replies. Training on examples and feedback helps it answer questions and follow instructions.

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:27.300
So: much more context, weights learned rather than counted, pieces of words, and extra training. Our paper model looks its numbers up; a large language model calculates them.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:34.020
But when it writes, it's still the loop you ran: pick the next token, write it down, go again.
